    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#barlas">Barlas</a> noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, carved out an empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> by conquest and calculated terror.</li><li>His heirs turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a> into brilliant centres of painting, poetry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> that shaped Safavid and Mughal culture.</li><li>Built on one man's authority, it split among feuding princes after 1405 and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a> carried its legacy to Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a> star catalogue of over a thousand stars was among the most accurate before the telescope, and was published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxford">Oxford</a> in 1665.</p></aside>
    </section>

